                "text": "I'm mapping out the template's grid coordinates and colored cell values, figuring out how the pattern needs to be rotated or reflected to align with each set of anchor points elsewhere in the grid.\n\nRecalling from the training example, there were two originals with distinct shapes, each corresponding to a different triple of anchor colors, and the original shapes get erased once copied onto their matching anchors. In the test grid, I identify two originals (one at rows3-6, another at rows9-11) and two anchor triples elsewhere, and now I need to work out which original matches which anchor set.\n\nComparing the relative offsets of colors 1, 4, 2 within each original against the offsets in each anchor triple, I find the second original's spacing (distance 2 between anchors) matches anchor set A. Working through the coordinate differences, I derive the linear transformation mapping the original's relative positions onto the anchor set's positions.\n\nI confirm the transform is a coordinate swap\u2014essentially a transpose\u2014since (1,0) maps to (0,1) and vice versa. Applying this transpose to all the relative cell positions from the second original grid, converting each (row, col) offset into (col, row), to build the transformed cell set relative to anchor 1.\n\nThen I place these transposed cells at the corresponding target anchor position, verifying against a separate anchor set to confirm the same transpose mapping holds consistently across different anchor triples.\n\nFinishing the offset mapping, then translating those relative positions to place the pattern at (19,10), which gives me the specific cell values to fill into the 24x19 grid.",
